WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.540
Picture a string of ponds across farmland.

2
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:12.340
Each pond holds a local population, the animals that live and breed there.

3
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.420
The fields between are hostile ground.

4
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.020
A set of such populations, linked by dispersal, is a metapopulation.

5
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.120
Ponds keep losing their populations.

6
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.520
Can a region keep a species when no single pond does?

7
00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:32.973
Richard Levins asked this in a simple way.

8
00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:36.333
He ignored how many animals each pond holds.

9
00:00:37.193 --> 00:00:39.853
Each patch is either occupied or empty.

10
00:00:40.913 --> 00:00:43.853
Call the fraction of patches that are occupied p.

11
00:00:45.293 --> 00:00:47.453
Occupied patches send out colonists.

12
00:00:48.673 --> 00:00:51.593
Colonists can found populations in empty patches.

13
00:00:52.653 --> 00:00:56.433
And each occupied patch has some chance of losing its population.

14
00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:01.227
Colonization has a rate, called c, per year.

15
00:01:01.987 --> 00:01:07.107
It needs colonists from occupied patches, and it works only on empty patches.

16
00:01:08.207 --> 00:01:11.247
Extinction has a rate, called e, per year.

17
00:01:12.087 --> 00:01:15.567
Each occupied patch loses its population at that rate.

18
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:21.847
Think of a row of lamps that fail at random and get relit from lamps still burning.

19
00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:27.067
The row stays partly lit as long as relighting outpaces failure.

20
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.360
Set the change to zero, and the occupied fraction settles at one minus e over c.

21
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:38.440
That is positive only when c is greater than e.

22
00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:44.240
Suppose c is 0.4 and e is 0.1 per year.

23
00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:47.500
The fraction settles at 0.75.

24
00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:53.580
Three patches in four are occupied at any time, though which ones keeps changing.

25
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.920
Now let e rise to 0.4, equal to c.

26
00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:03.600
The equilibrium falls to zero, and the network is lost.

27
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.160
But it is lost slowly.

28
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:14.000
Occupancy starts at 0.75 and falls by 0.225 per year at first.

29
00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.160
After 10 years, it is still 0.19.

30
00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:23.973
A network past its threshold can keep occupied patches for years.

31
00:02:24.353 --> 00:02:29.153
So seeing animals in some patches does not show that the region is persisting.

32
00:02:30.093 --> 00:02:31.153
The model is simple.

33
00:02:31.713 --> 00:02:36.553
It assumes identical patches, all equally reachable, with constant rates.

34
00:02:37.573 --> 00:02:41.573
Real networks have big and small patches, near and far ones.

35
00:02:42.083 --> 00:02:48.013
The rule to keep: a region persists when colonization outpaces local extinction.
